{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RCA CONFIRMED + REPAIR EVIDENCE (rebar-debug run)\n\n## Measurements re-verified at origin/main (3834269c53)\n\nF1 applier/invariants: `applier.py` yields 0 matches for the guard's three line prefixes.\n    DOUBLY vacuous — the facade imports siblings as `from rebar_reconciler.<mod> import ...`,\n    a spelling NONE of the three prefixes (`from .invariants`, `from invariants`,\n    `import invariants`) can match, so even an invariants import placed in applier.py itself\n    was invisible. The AC's subject, `inbound_repair_property`, lives in `apply_inbound.py`.\n    All \"invariants\" hits across the apply* family are docstrings/comments; 0 real imports.\nF2 llm wall-clock: `signal.alarm`/`Timer(` measured 0 across ALL of `src/rebar/` — the scan\n    of `runner.py` alone had no denominator; timeout machinery sits in `structured_run.py`\n    and `agent_call.py`.\nF3 probe neutrality: `inbound_probe.py` has 0 rebar imports of any kind.\nF4 usage-log colon split: `usage_log.py` has 0 banned string-parse calls. `_pricing_model_ref`\n    still happens to live there, so this one was aimed correctly but had no anti-vacuity\n    assertion — the next relocation disarms it exactly as F1-F3 were disarmed.\n\n## Repairs (test-only; no production behaviour changed)\n\nEach guard now DERIVES its scan population instead of pinning a filename, gains an\nanti-vacuity assertion on that population, and gains a teeth test driven through REAL\nmodules (not just a synthetic string — a synthetic-source test proves the predicate works\nbut cannot detect the guard being aimed at the wrong file, which is how all four survived).\n\nF1 -> applier facade + transitive closure of its intra-package imports; AST import scan\n      replaces line-prefix matching (catches the absolute spelling). Landmark: the\n      population must define `inbound_repair_property`.\nF2 -> runner + transitive closure of its intra-`rebar.llm` imports. Landmark: >= 2 scanned\n      modules must mention a timeout.\nF3 -> `inbound_probe` + transitive closure of its intra-package imports. Landmark: the\n      population must define `ProbeBranch` and `ProbeConfigError`.\nF4 -> population DERIVED from where `_pricing_model_ref`/`_price_row` are DEFINED (a\n      package glob was rejected: the wider `rebar.llm` package legitimately parses ':' in\n      provider registries and capability tables, and a guard that fires on those gets\n      deleted). `config.split_provider_qualifier` stays the sanctioned delegate.\nF6 -> relative `pathlib.Path(\"src/rebar/...\")` anchored to the imported module's `__file__`.\n      Proven: the old literal raises FileNotFoundError when pytest runs from any cwd but the\n      repo root; the guard now passes from `/tmp`.\n\n## Mutation checks (plant violation -> RED, remove -> GREEN). ALL FOUR PASS.\n\nF1: planted `from rebar_reconciler.invariants import check_schema` in `apply_inbound.py`\n    -> NEW guard RED (`apply_inbound:1`); OLD pinned guard GREEN (vacuous). Restored: GREEN.\nF2: planted a `Timer(` in `structured_run.py` -> NEW RED (`structured_run: Timer(`);\n    OLD GREEN (vacuous). Restored: GREEN.\nF3: split the vocabulary into a new `probe_vocab.py` importing `adapters.jira`\n    -> NEW RED (2 offenders); OLD GREEN (vacuous). Restored: GREEN.\nF4: relocated `_pricing_model_ref` into a sibling `pricing_ref.py` that colon-splits\n    -> NEW RED (`pricing_ref:3`); OLD GREEN (vacuous). Restored: GREEN.\n\n## Dispositions for the remaining findings\n\nF5 (`test_bedrock_provider.py`, capabilities.py prefix-matching) — FIXED, lightly. The\n   module still holds the exact-id tables, so the guard is aimed correctly; it lacked the\n   anti-vacuity assertion. Added a landmark requiring `_REBAR_OVERRIDES` and\n   `_MODEL_ID_CAPABILITY_OVERRIDES` to still be defined in the scanned module.\n\nF7 (`test_cloud_rate_limit_sweep_heldout.py` 4-file list vs `adapters/jira/backend.py`) —\n   ACCEPTED, with reason. `backend.py`'s `set_relationship` (:217) and `add_comment` (:271)\n   are ONE-LINE pure delegations to `self.transport.<same name>(...)`, explicitly sectioned\n   \"delegates to transport\". They issue no HTTP call and therefore carry no 429 policy of\n   their own; the rate-limit sweep is correctly scoped to the transport modules. NOT a\n   vacuity bug. The residual fragility is real but different in kind — the hardcoded list\n   would miss a NEW transport module — and closing it requires classifying every member of\n   the whole `adapters/jira/` package as mutating/non-mutating, which is a judgement task,\n   not a mechanical one. Filed as follow-up rather than forced into this bug.\n\nF8 (`test_container_roster.py` vs `plan_review/passes.py:403`) — ACCEPTED: FALSE POSITIVE of\n   the original sweep. `passes.py:403` renders per-child PROMPT SECTIONS\n   (`### child <id>: <title>\\n<description>`) and `det_floor.py:633` estimates TOKENS for a\n   parent+child pairing. Neither is the sibling roster (`- <id>: <title>`), and both already\n   carry descriptions — so neither exhibits the title-only defect the guard exists to\n   prevent. They merely share a substring with it."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785911591095552001,"uuid":"22b3fd46-f0e4-45cc-94f5-73a324e8d51d"}